e able to see  how they communicated if that  communication was so much better because  their children were getting right away  connections to the environment rather  than three years of delayed nothing no  language no fundamental language until  they got into school did you find any  families like that or is that something  you'd be interested in thank you  there are a few individuals who have  deaf relatives deaf aunts and so forth  and yes their language tends to be on a  par with what we would see in a  typically developing situation and you  absolutely have a rich environment  having a rich environment has a positive  impact on their language development one  thing I want to make sure that I  emphasize so often hearing parents who  have a deaf child have they're afraid  how will I learn sign language well  enough I can't possibly do that that's  not my language and that fear sometimes  drives them and there are multitude of  other reasons as well but it could drive  them to using spoken English  and I I didn't highlight this in my talk  but a lot of what we know is that  children are prodigious language  learners and they often will surpass  their input  so really the message that we want to  send to parents is that anything helps  learning sign with your kid helps their  language development that's what's  that's been shown so don't underestimate  what the kid has and what they can do  right and deaf families in Nicaragua are  quite rare I actually don't know of any  one who's taking a look at counting that  we know of a few but there aren't many I  feel like there's more here in the  United States but I could be wrong  I don't think there's ever been a count  done but in Nicaragua I certainly don't  know of many but a couple yes  okay last question I already spoke so  I'm happy to give up the mic to someone  who hasn't  why don't you speak so the interpreters  can hear you okay I'll just talk so I  have a couple questions my first one  being as two highly successful deaf  women how do you go about facing  prejudice in the workplace I mean that's  just really fascinating to me I'm a coda  and so I kind of have experience seeing  that prejudiced I'm just wondering I  mean I'm so so happy that you guys are  here I don't know I'm just interested in  that how do you would you advice about  counteracting that prejudice and things  like that  sorry that's so much yes it's okay in  many different ways all the time maybe  it would be helpful if you are curious  about if we're currently facing that  prejudice as adults or when we were  younger because my answer would be  different in those two contexts do you  mean you guys are both working at high  ranking institutions do you feel like  you still experience that okay sorry  that's a bad question it's fine okay  yes I can say a couple of things yes I  do experience things like that also I  have created a space for myself at my  current place of employment where I make  it work for me I make it that way  intentionally for myself and also I have  support from colleagues in my workplace  so to be honest I think I have a pretty  good place of employment where my  colleagues are supportive of me and my  work and they listen to what I have to  say about what I need and they basically  allow me to design the system there that  I want that works for me and I think  it's partly because well quite honestly  I don't know why that is but I do feel  that there are just good people there  just I'll briefly add one thing this  goes back to the intro of this panel I'm  lucky that I'm in the field that I'm in  that appreciates the importance of sign  language not only socially but on an  intellectual and academic level I'm also  lucky because my institution has a lot  of resources to support me in that work  but in that environment being in an  environment where people really are  appreciative of the study of sign  language like you all are that helps a  lot and that's fine and that's why I  really thank you all for hosting this  wonderful event thank you thank you  thank you very much  [Applause]  [Music]
 

